<edge source="global_energy_shocks::commercial_fuel_stocks" target="global_financial_shift::market_liquidity" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Larger commercial fuel inventories lower the probability of sudden supply shocks, reducing dealer risk aversion and inventory hoarding and thereby improving depth and bid-ask liquidity in sovereign bond markets.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_shocks</data>
  <data key="d40">global_financial_shift</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_shocks::commercial_fuel_stocks" target="russia_ukraine_war::military_escalation_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Higher commercially available stocks of fuel and refined products provide the immediate logistic fuel necessary for sustained mechanized operations and air sorties, enabling a higher rate of offensive or kinetic actions in the near term.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_shocks</data>
  <data key="d40">russia_ukraine_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="global_energy_shocks::commercial_fuel_stocks" target="us_iran_gulf_conflict::maritime_attack_rate" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.6</data>
  <data key="d37">Falling commercial fuel inventories raise the value of individual shipments and political incentives for state and proxy actors to interdict or harass vessels to extract concessions or raise prices, increasing the observed rate of maritime attacks; conversely higher stocks blunt that incentive.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">global_energy_shocks</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_gulf_conflict</data>
</edge>
